  "text": "None of the three answers matches the known threshold, as far as I can recall it.\n\n- **Solution 1 (q−1):** It asserts independence through weight q−1 and a relation at weight q, but gives no verifiable relation. Its appeal to Carlitz–Bernoulli degeneracy is hand-waving.\n- **Solution 2 (q+2):** Its valuation argument is the most concrete, but it only shows that leading terms are distinct up to weight q+2. Distinct leading valuations do not by themselves imply linear independence, since the argument has to handle all coefficients together. It then asserts a relation at weight q+3 \"as is known\" without naming one.\n- **Solution 3 (q):** Its dimension recurrence d(w) = d(w−1) + d(w−q) is attributed to Todd, but it is a count over K, not over F_q, so it cannot establish F_q-linear dependence. Its q=2 check at weight 3 relies on that same unjustified step.\n\nI could not verify the exact threshold from memory. My recollection is that the known counterexamples arise at a much larger weight, of order q² (possibly q²−q+1), not at q−1, q, q+1, or q+2. Since none of the proposed values is supported, I judge that no solution gives the correct answer. This is a judgment from memory and not a checked derivation, so treat it with that caveat.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
